course: dictionary
lesson: 7779
title: Words that travel
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have travelled a long way to reach you. Context helps you guess what they mean, and a word's story can tell you more. «Priya's grandma in Leicester smiles: "Pyjamas, shampoo and bungalow are Hindi words, beta!"» So a word can come from another language and still be part of everyday English.
explain: Pyjamas are loose, comfortable clothes for sleeping in, usually a top and trousers. Look at the clues in a sentence: «That night he dreams he has to fight Jake in front of the whole school, in his pyjamas.» Being in your pyjamas at school means you are not in your normal clothes, so the clothes are for bed.
explain: Pyjama can also come before another noun, like «pyjama party» or «pyjama top». Here it tells you what kind of party or top it is. To check a meaning, swap it into the sentence: a pyjama top is a top for sleeping in, and that makes sense.
fact: Pyjamas are loose, comfortable clothes for sleeping in, usually a top and trousers.
fact: «Pyjama» before another noun means belonging to pyjamas, as in «pyjama party» or «pyjama top».
fact: Priya's grandma says pyjamas is a Hindi word, one of several English words that came from other languages.
fact: «PJs» is a short, everyday way to say pyjamas.
fact: Pyjamas is usually a plural word, so we say «a pair of pyjamas» and «my pyjamas are warm».
exercise: «He changed out of his day clothes and put on his pyjamas before bed.» What are pyjamas? | Smart shoes for a party | *Loose, comfortable clothes for sleeping in | A type of school uniform | Warm coats for going outside
exercise: What does pyjama mean in «pyjama top»? | A sort of pastry | A loud, noisy party | *Belonging to pyjamas | A big black hat
exercise: Priya's grandma says pyjamas is a word from which language? | Welsh | Dutch | Latin | *Hindi
test: «Children with two homes often have two sets of pyjamas.» What does this tell us about pyjamas? | They are always red | *They are clothes you keep and wear, so you can have more than one set | They are only worn at school | They are a kind of food
test: You meet a word you do not know. What is a good way to check your meaning? | Guess and never check | Only look at the first letter | Ignore the sentence completely | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense
exam: «The bee's knees» and «the cat's pyjamas» were 1920s American slang. What did «the cat's pyjamas» mean? | A cat that wears clothes | *Something excellent | Something boring | Something wet and cold
